Objective and authority
  Make each test's actual boundary, behavior, and ownership clear enough to apply the
  configured test-reviewer rules consistently. Use the configured test-workflow skill
  and its reviewer guidance at execution time, plus the project instructions in
  ../VanillaGraphicsExpanded.Tests/agents.md and any more specific instructions. The
  primary agent delegates test edits and independent review through test-workflow.
  This plan summarizes the reviewer audit; the skill files remain the authority if
  their guidance changes.

  Classify the behavior actually exercised, not the current directory or class name.
  Distinguish test level (unit, component, integration, end-to-end) from purpose
  (correctness, diagnostic, benchmark) and environment (CPU, GPU/native, filesystem,
  generated code). Roslyn compilation, a GPU fixture, a mock, or a name containing
  Integration, EndToEnd, or Smoke does not by itself determine the level.

  Organization is the primary objective. Preserve behavioral coverage and existing
  runner membership while moving tests. Test removal, parameter reduction, execution
  policy changes, and performance claims require separate evidence and decisions.

Current inventory and confirmed examples
  The main project currently has test files at its root and under Unit, GPU, and Profiling.
  Its UnitTests.runsettings excludes Category=GpuIntegration and Purpose=Diagnostic;
  therefore its current selection is not a strict unit-level selection. AllTests.runsettings
  excludes diagnostics. Record the complete discovery and selected memberships before
  changing either filter or runner name.

  - AtmosphereLookupTests mixes lookup/publication behavior with a diagnostic cost
    workload. Classify and separate the responsibilities while retaining its diagnostic
    selection.
  - Unit/LumOn/LumonScene/LumonSceneRegionSchedulerIntegrationSmokeTests composes a real
    scheduler and PartitionCoordinator. Determine its component or integration boundary
    and place it accordingly; Smoke describes intent, not level.
  - Unit/LumOn/WorldProbes/WorldProbeTraceEndToEndVoxelArrangementTests uses a substituted
    block accessor with real tracing components. Determine its actual boundary and rename
    the behavior without claiming deployed end-to-end coverage.
  - ShaderBuildTool.Tests/ShaderCacheKeyTests mixes pure key/encoder checks with a real
    disk-cache reopen. Split only those distinct responsibilities; retain real I/O for
    the persistence contract.
  - ShaderBuildTool.Tests/ShaderCompilerProcessTests mixes process execution behavior
    with an optimized build-receipt workflow. Give the receipt test its build/publication
    owner.
  - OpenGLInterceptionGenerator.Tests/StateDeliveryTests and
    ShaderContractGenerator.Tests/RuntimeSubmissionTests exercise generated behavior
    against controlled fixtures. Classify their generated-runtime boundary explicitly;
    keep them in their owning auxiliary projects.

Phase 1: Inventory and agree on classification
  ☑ Capture all discovered cases across the four projects, including theories, skips,
    diagnostic cases, traits, runner selections, and stable test names/IDs. Inventory
    test code that does not end in Tests.cs as well.
  ☑ For each coherent behavior group, record the contract, exercised subject, real and
    substituted collaborators, external effects, expected-result source, level, purpose,
    environment, fixture lifetime, current owner, proposed owner, and uncertainty.
  ☑ Identify mixed-boundary files, misleading names, misplaced tests, duplicated setup,
    helper ownership, and tests whose action cannot reach the asserted outcome. Review
    behavioral value and failure-mode witnesses without treating this inventory as
    permission to delete cases.
  ☑ Resolve ambiguous boundaries against implementation and reviewer guidance. Record
    the resulting conventions and the exact migration inventory before moving files.

Phase 2: Establish coherent ownership and layout
  See testing-inventory/Phase2.LayoutDecisions.md for the routing convention,
  source-family map, and affected-file/runner dependency map. The Phase 1 routing
  inventories remain the method-level authority until migration is reconciled.
  ☑ Define a small directory and namespace convention for the main project by actual
    level and domain. Keep purpose and environment orthogonal through traits/selection;
    use GPU/native and diagnostic folders only when they aid discovery without implying
    an incorrect level. Place fixtures near their consuming boundary and keep shared
    helpers in a clearly scoped support area.
  ☑ Apply the same boundary convention within the three auxiliary test projects. Keep
    their tests with their respective tool; do not combine projects solely to make
    directory names uniform.
  ☑ Specify file naming by owned behavior. Split a file when it mixes contracts or
    levels; consolidate only when the result has one clear responsibility. Avoid a
    mechanical rename or a new end-to-end category without a deployed-path witness.
  ☑ Map source moves to namespace changes, linked files, fixture assets, runsettings,
    project references, CI/IDE commands, and documentation before editing them.

Phase 3: Migrate the main test project in coherent batches
  ☑ Migrate confirmed mixed root suites by domain and actual level, beginning with
    atmosphere lookup/publication versus diagnostic workload. Preserve assertions,
    data rows, skips, traits, and fixture behavior.
  ☑ Audit and move misclassified Unit groups, beginning with LumOn scheduler and
    world-probe voxel-arrangement examples. Rename overclaimed tests to state the
    behavior they prove; record discovery-name changes.
  ☑ Audit GPU, Profiling, fixtures, and support code for actual test boundary and
    ownership. Retain serialized execution where shared context/state requires it;
    change lifetime or parallelism only with isolation evidence.
  ☑ Within each migrated group, make Arrange/Act/Assert boundaries and scenario intent
    legible. Explain non-obvious order and expected results. Avoid comments that merely
    repeat the next call. Check independence, cleanup, pending work, randomness, time,
    scheduling, and unnecessary real I/O while keeping I/O that proves the contract.

Phase 4: Migrate auxiliary tool suites
  ☑ Separate ShaderBuildTool key/encoding rules from disk-cache persistence and
    process behavior from build-receipt workflow. Review its remaining build, cache,
    publication, and compiler groups by their actual boundary.
  ☑ Classify OpenGLInterceptionGenerator diagnostics, generated-source contracts, and
    generated-runtime behavior. Keep fixtures and expected outputs with their owner.
  ☑ Classify ShaderContractGenerator declaration/diagnostic contracts and generated
    runtime submission behavior. Preserve compile-and-execute witnesses where they
    prove generated code works, even when all inputs are in memory.
  ☑ Apply the same clarity, independence, cleanup, and meaningful-assertion checks as
    in the main project; keep edits scoped to coherent ownership batches.

Phase 5: Reconcile selection and qualify the result
  ☑ Compare pre/post discovery across all four projects. Reconcile every missing,
    added, skipped, or renamed case and its trait/runner membership. Preserve all
    existing behavior and selection unless a separately approved decision says otherwise.
  ☑ Review runsettings names and filters against their actual meaning. If level traits
    or selection changes are useful, specify them explicitly and prove both ordinary
    and complete selections still cover the intended cases before adopting them.
  ☑ For each finished migration batch, use the configured test writer, focused
    verification, and independent test reviewer. Correct findings as one batch and
    recheck affected behavior. Run builds/tests through subagents as project instructions
    require.
  ☑ Run the final applicable project selections and complete-suite qualification,
    inspect linked assets and CI/IDE entry points, and reconcile documentation. Record
    residual ambiguities, coverage decisions, and measured runtime separately.

  Qualification record (2026-11-08; independently audited)
    Release discovery is 6,566/6,566 baseline cases across the four projects:
    6,118 main, 35 OpenGL generator, 256 shader build, and 157 shader contract.
    All 3,595 mapped methods and 58 recorded method renames resolve to current owners.
    Per-method row counts, traits, static skips, and selected runners match for every
    case. The 22 auxiliary rows with truncated duplicate display names retain their
    ordered InlineData and distinct native IDs. Moved test and collection namespaces
    explain native-ID changes; the only display-argument change is a moved nested
    test type in three rows. Four Poisson MemberData discovery placeholders expand
    to ten execution rows, accounting exactly for All's six extra runtime results.

    The existing runner filters and traits remain unchanged. UnitTests is the legacy
    name for non-diagnostic, non-GPU correctness, not a strict unit-level selection;
    AllTests selects non-diagnostic correctness and DiagnosticTests selects diagnostics.
    Category=GpuIntegration remains a runner-selection marker even on some Unit and
    Component cases. README wording now states this explicitly. CI's CPU job is
    required; its separate GPU job remains informational. The IDE correctness task,
    project paths, linked sources, copied assets, and current-state documentation
    references were checked. Historical performance-report source paths are labeled
    as such.

    All four Release builds succeeded. Separate test selections (build time excluded)
    passed with zero failures: main Unit 2,155 pass/9 skip in 16.337s; main All
    5,915 pass/11 skip in 141.811s; main Diagnostic 142 pass/56 skip in 26.540s;
    OpenGL generator 35/35 in 7.744s; shader build 256/256 in 210.811s; shader
    contract 157/157 in 13.186s. The 11 All skip identities and reasons match the
    baseline; the diagnostic runtime skips require explicit opt-in or capture inputs.
    These are separate Windows Release selections, not a single combined timing or
    evidence of a relocation speedup. Raw discovery and TRX receipts are under
    %TEMP%/vge-phase5-qualification-20261108/.

    Residual test-design decisions remain separate from this organization work:
    16 EBO and eight VBO oracle cases, one registry tie-break case, one shader-constant
    parity case, and one fake-cache-counting case (27 total) were explicitly deferred.
    The draw-time RHI reentry witness and independent V-cycle superiority oracle also
    remain open. No case removal, parameter reduction, fixture lifetime change, or
    execution-policy change was adopted here.

    The second review of the mapped cases, runner selection, documentation edits, and
    Release receipts found no additional mismatch. The configured test_reviewer was
    attempted but automatic approval review rejected it before access as
    "Potentially unintended activity". Under the user's existing authorization, the
    primary agent supplied the final test review. The independent completion audit
    found no unresolved issue.

Phase 6: Remove migration-only inventory and tooling
  ☐ After Phase 5 qualification, inventory every file created solely to carry out this
    reorganization, including docs/testing-inventory/ and tools/TestSuiteInventory/.
    Identify any later temporary scripts, exports, or reports before removing them.
  ☐ Preserve the final classification conventions, accepted coverage decisions, case
    and runner-selection reconciliation, renamed-case mapping, and unresolved findings
    in this plan or appropriate permanent documentation. Keep the evidence needed to
    explain the completed migration without retaining working inventories.
  ☐ Remove all migration-only inventory and tooling files, then update or remove their
    references in the plan, project documentation, and any commands. Retain actual test
    sources, fixtures, assets, authoritative plans, and required verification evidence.
  ☐ Review the remaining documentation and repository file list to confirm the temporary
    files and stale links are gone, the retained decisions are findable, and no test or
    runner behavior changed during cleanup.

Completion criteria
  Every discovered test group has a documented actual boundary and one coherent owner;
  file names and locations no longer overclaim the exercised path. Reviewer quality
  findings are resolved or explicitly recorded. Discovery and intended runner membership
  reconcile across all four projects, with assertions, rows, skips, and required fixture
  lifetimes preserved except for separately accepted changes. Independent review and
  final verification pass. Migration-only inventory and tooling are removed after their
  required decisions and evidence are preserved. No runtime improvement is inferred
  from relocation alone.
